<BaseLayout
  title="Home"
  description="SIGRUN, a capability-based microkernel in Rust for x86_64 — plain-English interview study guide with diagrams."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Overview · index</p>
  <h1>SIGRUN — a capability-based microkernel, explained in plain words</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    SIGRUN is a hobby operating system written in Rust for x86_64. It boots from GRUB into 64-bit long
    mode, sets up paging, a preemptive scheduler, timers, interrupts, IPC, and a capability system — and
    it is honest about what is finished and what is a scaffold.
  </p>

  <p>
    The whole kernel is <strong>~15,000 lines of Rust across 82 files</strong>. This guide walks every
    subsystem with one diagram, one plain-English explanation, one annotated snippet, and the interview
    questions that go with it. Read it top-to-bottom once, then drill into whatever an interviewer pokes at.
  </p>

  <div class="pitch">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>What it is</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Microkernel: <em>memory, scheduling, IPC, capabilities</em> in the kernel; everything else in userspace.</li>
        <li>Capability-based: every resource is an unforgeable, rights-carrying reference — no global "permissions table".</li>
        <li>Rust: <code class="mono">unsafe</code> only where hardware demands it (assembly, MMIO, register access).</li>
        <li>Real ring-3 path: a user process <em>does</em> run via <code class="mono">iretq</code> and calls real <code class="mono">syscall</code>/<code class="mono">sysretq</code>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>What actually works today</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="badge work">WORKING</span> Boot: GRUB2 → multiboot2 → long mode</li>
        <li><span class="badge work">WORKING</span> Buddy frame allocator, 4-level paging</li>
        <li><span class="badge work">WORKING</span> Preemptive round-robin scheduler (10 ms LAPIC timer)</li>
        <li><span class="badge work">WORKING</span> Ring-3 demo process + SYSCALL/SYSRET + serial <code>write</code></li>
        <li><span class="badge scaf">SCAFFOLD</span> Channels, notifications, shared memory, capability tables (data structures + syscall stubs; kernel-internal demo only)</li>
        <li><span class="badge scaf">SCAFFOLD</span> Userspace crates (filesystem, network, shell…) compile on host, never spawned</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

