  <h2>The honest status box</h2>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <span class="label">Read this before any interview</span>
    Do not oversell. The kernel boots, preempts, switches context, and runs a ring-3 demo that calls
    <em>real</em> syscalls — that is an honest achievement. But: the userspace crates under
    <code class="mono">userspace/</code> are compiled for the host and never spawned; the VFS, network stack,
    and shell return hardcoded data; <code class="mono">boot/</code> is an abandoned UEFI path. Interviews
    reward the person who can <strong>say exactly what works and what is next</strong>, not the person who
    claims a finished OS. The start-of-boot log is roughly: validate boot params → memory manager →
    GDT/IDT/APIC → timer calibration → scheduler → capabilities → IPC → SYSCALL setup → kernel tasks →
    ring-3 init demo → idle loop.
  </div>

  <h2>One-minute pitch</h2>
  <p>
    "SIGRUN is a microkernel I wrote in Rust to understand what sits between an <code class="mono">if
    let</code> and the bare metal. It boots through GRUB2's multiboot2 protocol: my own assembly
    transitions from 32-bit protected mode to 64-bit long mode using 1&nbsp;GB huge pages, then calls Rust.
    The kernel owns four things — a buddy frame allocator plus 4-level paging, a preemptive round-robin
    scheduler driven by a PIT-calibrated LAPIC timer at 10&nbsp;ms, interrupt plumbing (GDT/IDT/APIC with a
    double-fault IST), and a capability-based IPC layer where every channel endpoint, notification, and
    shared-memory region is referenced by a rights-carrying capability. I also implemented SYSCALL/SYSRET
    and demonstrated a real ring-3 process printing to the serial console through a syscall. The honest
    gaps are that the userspace servers are still scaffolds — they compile, but nothing has spawned them yet."
  </p>

