  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Starter interview questions</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">WARMUP</span>Why did you write an operating system from scratch?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          To close the loop between application code and hardware: memory allocation, context switching,
          interrupts, and privilege levels are usually abstracted away, and I wanted to own them. It is also
          the densest way to learn Rust's <code>unsafe</code> model — every <code>unsafe</code> block in the
          kernel has a concrete hardware justification (register access, MMIO, paging structures), which is
          exactly what "correct over fast" means in practice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">WARMUP</span>What is the difference between a microkernel and a monolithic kernel, and where does SIGRUN sit?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          A monolithic kernel (Linux) runs drivers, filesystems, and network stacks in the kernel with direct
          hardware access; a microkernel moves those services to user-space processes that talk over IPC, so
          a driver crash takes down the driver, not the kernel. SIGRUN sits firmly on the microkernel side:
          the kernel provides memory, scheduling, IPC, and capabilities, and the design puts filesystem,
          network, driver management, and the shell in separate <em>servers</em>. The trade-off is IPC cost
          and complexity — which is why SIGRUN invests in capability-carried channel endpoints and shared
          memory as the fast path.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">WARMUP</span>What does "capability-based" mean in one sentence?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          There is no central access-control list: a process can only touch an object if it holds a capability
          for it — a handle the kernel minted — and the capability itself carries the rights (read/write/grant),
          so rights can be narrowed by derivation and withdrawn by revocation (see
          <a href="/capabilities">Capabilities</a>).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

